{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference of future truth, but as a critical selection mechanism for 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) within a non-justificationist framework; however, accepting this costs the scientist any guarantee that the selected theory is actually closer to the truth, reducing rational action to a gamble on the severity of past tests rather than a probability of success.","counterconditions":["If the Tichý/Miller proof holds that verisimilitude cannot be consistently defined for false theories, then the preference lacks a coherent target.","If Popper's own practical choices relied on an implicit belief in the uniformity of nature (induction), then the distinction is merely semantic.","If 'preference' implies an expectation of future performance, it functionally reintroduces probability regardless of the label."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between 'corroboration' (a logical report of past test severity) and 'probability' (an inductive prediction). It posits that preferring high corroboration is a methodological rule to maximize verisimilitude without claiming knowledge of it. The mechanism relies on the asymmetry of falsification: while we cannot know a theory is true, we can know it has survived tests that would have killed a false one, making it the 'best bet' available without invoking induction.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}